In this lesson we'll look at one of the most useful facts in geometry: the three interior angles of any triangle always add up to one hundred and eighty degrees. Picture any triangle you like. At each of its three corners there's an angle, and if you imagine tearing the triangle apart so those three angles come together at a single point, with their tips touching, they line up perfectly into a straight line. Since a straight line measures one hundred and eighty degrees, the three angles must sum to one hundred and eighty. This works for every triangle, no matter its shape or size. If you want a more rigorous version, you can draw a line through one vertex running parallel to the opposite side. The two new angles you create there match two of the triangle's interior angles, and the third angle sits between them, all on a straight line, again totalling one hundred and eighty degrees. From this, a few quick consequences fall out. A triangle can't have two right angles, since that alone would already be one hundred and eighty. It also can't have two obtuse angles. And if two angles are equal, the third has to be one hundred and eighty minus that common value. One important reminder, this rule only works in degrees, the unit we use for angles in the plane. Now let's see it in action.